How does a university reporting application access Microsoft 365, and which records can show its activity?

In investigation mode it can be written in diffrent way:

1.	Evidence to Examine:
Procedure or basic explanation: University reporting application is a software application that collects, organizes and records present information of staff members, faculties and students enrolled in university. University stores attendance, fees, marks, documents and various things in Microsoft 365 services such as OneDrive, SharePoint, Outlook, etc. 
There must be some controlled authorization and protected rules for any university to get access over the data that they are storing. For Microsoft 365 application, the university is the third-party organization, that raises a question about how Microsoft 365 software knows that the third-party organization should be trusted.  
The University registers its application in Microsoft ID. Microsoft application gives client or user ID, which identifies the application. The application gets its recognition/ credentials that proves its identity. The university when asks for permission to access any resource of Microsoft 365 then an interface plays role; Microsoft Graph API. 
The application uses Microsoft Graph API to request information or documents from Microsoft 365. Access is allowed according to the token and applicable permissions. The application authenticates with the help of Microsoft Entra ID. If it succeeds, Entra ID can access the token that grants the access of the information required. 
All the activities happening, whether it's storing, arrangement of data or request to access it getting permitted or denied, everything is maintained under logs. There are four types of logs; Sign-in logs, Audit logs, Graph activity logs, Unified Audit logs. Logs are digital records that shows which record can show the activity is requested by third party organization. 
Sign-in log keeps record of whether user’s authentication is successful, IP address of user signing in, and User ID. Audit logs keeps record of the modifications in permissions, registrations and configurations. Graph activity logs keep record of API requests. Lastly, Unified audit logs keep track of the changes and details of the services used under Microsoft 365 software.
In the investigation the investigator should examine the logs in this manner; Audit logs to keep track about how and when new credential was added. Proceeding with checking Sign-in logs to check if application was sign in after new credential was successful. And with that credential were requests made?

2.	Interpretation and limitations
During the investigation all the records can be shown by the sequence of activities occurred, investigator can assume that the application authenticated after the credential change, if the audit logs show that the new credential was added and the sign in logs shows authentication is successful.
However, the sign of authentication is successful does not necessarily mean that the application accessed the SharePoint or One drive document. Investigator can confirm by checking SharePoint or OneDrive audits logs such as file was downloaded, modifies or edited and match the timestamp and identity. 
While interpreting these records investigator may found some limitations, such as if logging was not enabled or it was expired due to accessing limits then logs may not be available anymore. 
An IP address can help in identifying the source of a request, but the person operating on the application can not be identified. Similarly, permission granted to an application does not prove that the permission was used.

3.	Practical implication for the investigator
Investigator should go through logs records, Audit records to check when and who added new credential. Sign-in logs and Unified logs are needed most.
every original activity should be checked cause if a document permission was granted, we don’t have information about whether document was used or opened. Timestamp, identity of user, registration, configuration everything is needed to check. Until every requirement of information is not found investigator shouldn’t conclude.
